WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.020
Three sensory-neuron atlases report eleven, seventeen and eighteen groups.

2
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:07.500
Which count should you trust?

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.700
Watch the same question change as the evidence changes.

4
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:18.433
Each dot is one mouse dorsal root ganglion neuron.

5
00:00:19.713 --> 00:00:22.433
Its measured RNA becomes a teal barcode.

6
00:00:23.653 --> 00:00:26.053
A clustering method groups similar barcodes.

7
00:00:27.473 --> 00:00:33.113
Similar barcodes suggest similar molecular programs, but they do not reveal a job by

8
00:00:33.113 --> 00:00:38.693
themselves. The result is a map of this sample, not a finished catalogue of nature.

9
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:43.507
Now keep every neuron and every RNA count unchanged.

10
00:00:44.827 --> 00:00:46.707
Turn the clustering resolution higher.

11
00:00:48.007 --> 00:00:51.927
Will the tree gain neurons, lose neurons, or split existing branches?

12
00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.390
It splits branches.

13
00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:01.910
No new neuron appeared.

14
00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.430
That simple dial shows how resolution can change a count.

15
00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:13.450
In real atlases, one mouse data set resolved eleven classes, and a larger census

16
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:14.730
resolved seventeen.

17
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:21.470
A cross-species integration later reported eighteen dorsal root ganglion subtypes.

18
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.130
Those counts also differ in sample and method.

19
00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:26.130
. . . .

20
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.560
Resolution is only one control.

21
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.640
Whole cells and nuclei capture different RNA, so preparation can hide a branch.

22
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.900
A sample can also miss rare cells before clustering even begins.

23
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:45.880
Injury can pull several established types into one red transcriptional state.

24
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.120
Neither change is simply extra zoom.

25
00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:53.813
A transcriptomic cluster is therefore a candidate type.

26
00:01:55.013 --> 00:01:59.433
It earns a functional type claim when it recurs and an independent measurement

27
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:05.433
anchors it. Its count and boundary still depend on resolution, preparation and

28
00:02:05.433 --> 00:02:06.433
state.

29
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:09.677
Genetic access supplies that test in mice.

30
00:02:11.017 --> 00:02:16.017
A marker from the teal RNA profile drives a gold label in the candidate cells.

31
00:02:17.297 --> 00:02:22.717
If one marker is too broad, requiring two expression histories narrows the label to

32
00:02:22.717 --> 00:02:23.717
their intersection.

33
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:29.740
Researchers can follow those labelled endings into skin, then stimulate the skin

34
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:31.180
while imaging the ganglion.

35
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.460
In one toolkit, labelled subtypes covered different tested ranges of force and

36
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:43.460
temperature. A distinct ending or response range is independent of the clustering

37
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:48.880
settings. It strengthens the type claim without making every cell identical.

38
00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:51.533
The test still has limits.

39
00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:57.533
A driver records marker expression, sometimes during a chosen time window.

40
00:02:58.993 --> 00:03:01.193
Calcium signals do not count every spike.

41
00:03:02.413 --> 00:03:07.933
The result supports a functional type only within the preparation, stimuli and cells

42
00:03:07.933 --> 00:03:08.933
actually tested.

43
00:03:09.917 --> 00:03:11.817
So do not ask which atlas won.

44
00:03:13.197 --> 00:03:17.617
Ask what produced each boundary, then ask whether another measurement reaches the

45
00:03:17.617 --> 00:03:23.537
same group. That turns a cluster from a pattern into a testable biological claim.
